1. Compact Source Files and Instance main() Methods — Final in Java 25
=======================================================================
Problem before: Even a small Java program needed a class and a traditional main() method.

Before:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello Java");
    }
}

After Java 25:

void main() {
    System.out.println("Hello Java");
}

Why useful? Beginners can write small programs with less boilerplate. Java can implicitly declare a class for this kind of source file.

Interview answer: “Java 25 finalized compact source files and instance main methods, making it easier to start writing Java programs without the usual class and static main boilerplate.”

Note: this simplifies small programs; normal classes and public static void main(String[] args) remain valid.

2. Module Import Declarations — Final in Java 25
================================================
Problem before: When using classes from different packages, we often write several import statements.

Before:

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = List.of("Riya", "Amit");
        System.out.println(names);
    }
}

After Java 25:

import module java.base;

void main() {
    List<String> names = List.of("Riya", "Amit");
    System.out.println(names);
}

Why useful? import module imports the public packages exported by a module, reducing the need for individual import statements.

Interview answer: “Module Import Declarations, finalized in Java 25, allow us to import all exported packages of a module with a single declaration.” 
