3. Flexible Constructor Bodies — Final in Java 25
=====================================================
Problem before: In a subclass constructor, an explicit super(...) or this(...) call generally had to come before other statements.

Before:

class Person {
    Person(String name) {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}

class Employee extends Person {
    Employee(String name) {
        super(validate(name));
    }

    static String validate(String name) {
        if (name == null || name.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Name required");
        }
        return name;
    }
}

After Java 25:

class Person {
    Person(String name) {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}

class Employee extends Person {
    Employee(String name) {
        if (name == null || name.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Name required");
        }

        super(name);
    }
}

Why useful? We can validate constructor arguments before calling the parent constructor, without always needing a helper method.

Interview answer: “Flexible Constructor Bodies allow certain statements before super() or this(), improving constructor validation and initialization. Those statements cannot access the object under construction through this.” 
