4. Primitive Types in Patterns, instanceof, and switch — Preview in Java 25
===========================================================================
Problem before: Pattern matching mainly focused on reference types, and primitive values often required separate handling.

Traditional code:

int value = 100;

if (value > 0) {
    System.out.println("Positive");
} else {
    System.out.println("Zero or negative");
}

Java 25 preview syntax can use primitive type patterns in supported pattern contexts. For example:

Object value = 100;

// Java 25 preview feature
if (value instanceof int number) {
    System.out.println(number);
}

Why useful? It aims to make pattern matching more consistent across primitive and reference types.

Interview answer: “Java 25 previewed primitive type patterns for instanceof and switch, extending pattern matching to primitive types.”

This is a preview feature, so it requires preview-feature compilation and runtime flags. It is not a finalized Java 25 feature. 
