5. Scoped Values — Final in Java 25
=====================================
Problem before: We may use ThreadLocal to share data such as a user ID across methods running on the same thread.

Before:

private static final ThreadLocal<String> USER =
        new ThreadLocal<>();

static void processRequest() {
    USER.set("Riya");

    try {
        service();
    } finally {
        USER.remove();
    }
}

static void service() {
    System.out.println(USER.get());
}

After Java 25:

private static final ScopedValue<String> USER =
        ScopedValue.newInstance();

static void processRequest() {
    ScopedValue.where(USER, "Riya").run(() -> service());
}

static void service() {
    System.out.println(USER.get());
}

Add this import for the example:

import java.lang.ScopedValue;

Why useful? Scoped values share data within a bounded execution scope without the usual mutable ThreadLocal setup and cleanup pattern.

Interview answer: “Scoped Values became final in Java 25 and provide a safer, scoped way to share immutable data across method calls, especially useful with virtual threads and concurrent tasks.” 
