7. Key Derivation Function (KDF) API — Final in Java 25
================================================================
Problem before: Java developers implementing cryptographic workflows often needed provider-specific APIs or external libraries for key derivation.

A key derivation function creates a cryptographic key from secret material and other inputs.

Conceptually:

// Simplified workflow — not complete cryptographic code

byte[] secret = obtainSecret();
byte[] salt = obtainSalt();

// Derive a key using a secure KDF algorithm.
byte[] derivedKey = deriveKey(secret, salt);

Java 25 introduces a standard KDF API so applications can use supported key derivation algorithms through a common Java interface.

Why useful? It helps developers build cryptographic applications with standardized key-derivation support.

Interview answer: “Java 25 finalized the Key Derivation Function API, providing a standard API for deriving cryptographic keys from secret material.”

For production security, use a vetted algorithm and correct parameters rather than implementing cryptography yourself. 
